Kevin Wayne wrote:

> I guess what really annoys me is the game asking if I really want to
> do what I just told it I want to do. Granted, this behavior prevents a
> fingerslip from causing me to attack a peaceful, but in the case of
> looting a chest or kicking a door, it appears to be too cautionary.
> The player has already had to supply a direction, which should be
> warning enough if kicking or looting wasn't actually what you wanted
> to do.

> I also understand that there could be more than one lootable container
> on a square, in which case the game needs to determine which container
> you want to loot. But I would think it would be relatively easy to
> determine if there is only one, and then to default to it, rather than
> asking for a [y,n] response.

Then we'd get the same problem as with the T and R commands: if you're
only wearing ONE appropriate piece of equipment, the game does not ask you
which one you want to take off or remove.

This can easily lead to errors, if you're accustomed to typing, say, "Tg"
to take off your gloves. If you're only wearing gloves, the "g" is
interpreted as a prefix for the next move command.

I think that every command should always be followed by the same,
predictable, number of extra characters.

So, no matter the number of containers on a spot, I think the games should
always ask - for each container - whether you want to loot it or not (even
if there's only one containter there).

